{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PLAN FACTS VERIFIED IN THE TREE — no defect found, and one CROSS-TICKET TRAP the implementer must\nnot walk into. Checked at 47b9a511871.\n\nEVERYTHING THE PLAN ASSERTS IS TRUE:\n * adapters/jira_family/value_maps.py EXISTS (3.3K) and already holds LOCAL_PRIORITY_TO_JIRA (:22)\n   and LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA (:49), so the canonical home the plan names is real and the ticket-type\n   map has a natural place beside them.\n * The J2 alias-import precedent is where the plan says, outbound_fields.py:35-36:\n       from rebar_reconciler.adapters.jira_family import LOCAL_PRIORITY_TO_JIRA as _LOCAL_TO_JIRA_PRIORITY\n       from rebar_reconciler.adapters.jira_family import LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA  as _LOCAL_TO_JIRA_STATUS\n * Both one-literal guards exist and are the shape to copy:\n   test_exactly_one_status_map_literal_within_adapters (:296) and\n   test_exactly_one_priority_map_literal_within_adapters (:306).\n\n*** THE TRAP: USE AN ABSOLUTE IMPORT HERE, NOT A RELATIVE ONE — AND DO NOT CARRY THIS IDIOM OVER TO\ncc77. ***\nThe J2 precedent's own comment states the reason for the absolute form, and it is load-bearing:\n'this module is loaded via spec_from_file_location with NO PACKAGE CONTEXT in several tests, where a\nRELATIVE IMPORT WOULD RAISE ImportError.' So copying J2 exactly — absolute — is correct for this\nticket, and 'tidying' it to a relative import would break test loading.\n\nThat sits in DIRECT TENSION with sibling ticket cc77-8120-bcc6-47e8, which established (and I\nre-verified independently) that mypy WIDENS anything imported through the top-level\n'rebar_reconciler.' name to Any, because that name is a runtime sys.path injection rather than an\nimportable distribution, and ignore_missing_imports is set globally. Both facts are true at once and\nthey do not conflict, because they are about DIFFERENT THINGS:\n  * THIS ticket imports a DICT. Nothing is attribute-checked, so Any-widening costs nothing and the\n    runtime import-form requirement (spec_from_file_location compatibility) wins. Absolute is right.\n  * cc77 imports a TYPE used in ANNOTATIONS. There, Any-widening silently destroys the entire\n    enforcement the ticket exists to add, so a resolvable (relative) import is mandatory.\nStating it here because the two tickets are in the same epic and an implementer who reads one and\nthen the other could plausibly 'unify the import style' and silently break either the tests (going\nrelative here) or the type enforcement (going absolute there). Neither is a style question.\n\nAlso already recorded on this ticket: the two _LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE copies are CONTENT-IDENTICAL, so\nthe unification is behaviour-preserving and needs no divergence decision."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785700017894612001,"uuid":"eebb12e1-a506-4994-86a2-3942164a8037"}